<plan-hardening-step id="{{ step }}" runner="{{ runner }}">
  <phase>{{ phase }}</phase>
  <root>{{ root }}</root>
  <instructions><![CDATA[
Your instructions are the description of step bead {{ step }}. Run every shell
command from `{{ workdir }}` with `BEADS_DIR={{ beads_dir }}` and
`TMPDIR={{ scratch_dir }}`. Read repository plan data, reviewer files, scripts and configuration only
from that workdir or beads directory; the explicitly supplied plan index and
run artifacts are read from scratch. Never read another checkout. Write every evidence and temporary file only under the exact directory
`{{ scratch_dir }}/{{ step }}/`; create it before starting. Never use the
agent session scratchpad. The selected plan index and tool-owned receipts are
separate allowed plan writes; sprint-review writes its DAG under the clone
`docs/plans/phase-{{ phase }}/` as well as its scratch outputs. The
sprint-review cache must be `{{ cache_path }}`: verify the Git common directory
and cache resolve within the workdir before running it. The dispatcher must
pass these exact values to every subagent or teammate; never infer them.
{% if plan_pr == 'none' %}This run has no plan PR: never push or commit the DAG, even if the step
description says to push; leave its generated artifact in this disposable workdir and copy evidence to scratch.
{% else %}Update the plan PR as instructed by the step description.
{% endif %}
{% if plan_index != 'integration' %}For validate-plan and sprint-review, pass `--index '{{ plan_index }}'` explicitly.
{% else %}For validate-plan and sprint-review, use the integration-branch index.
{% endif %}Save each validate-plan and sprint-review command, its complete output and
exit status immediately to a named scratch path; list the commands, paths and
exit codes in the returned `data.commands` array. Never silence or discard stderr for evidence-bearing commands: capture it
with stdout in the step evidence directory, including filing, `bd` reads/writes,
sc-compose, dependency edits, ATM closes and receipts; retain their exit codes.
Do not redirect these streams to `/dev/null` or ignore command failures.
The checker cannot establish that an agent reported every command, so this is
a runner obligation even when the reported evidence passes validation.
Also list every `bd` write
in `data.bd_writes` and every written file in `data.file_writes`; use empty
arrays when none occurred so the dispatcher can audit the run. If validate-plan fails specifically
because `bd doctor` reports `proxy.doctor.unsupported`, retain the verbatim
error and rerun that same command once with `--no-doctor`; record the fallback
and original error in the close reason or failure envelope (atm-bd-orchestration
#807). Any other doctor error fails the step; any other nonzero exit is a
failed step, not a plan finding or a passing result. This template starts, records and
closes the step.

For evidence-bearing commands, use this portable capture idiom, once per
command with a distinct name; it records output and exit status and preserves
the command's failure for the caller (no shell-specific PIPESTATUS):

```bash
capture() {
  name=$1; shift
  if "$@" >"$TMPDIR/$name.out" 2>&1; then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi
  cat "$TMPDIR/$name.out"
  printf '%s\n' "$rc" >"$TMPDIR/$name.exit"
  return "$rc"
}
```

1. Start:

```bash
set -euo pipefail
cd '{{ workdir }}'
export BEADS_DIR='{{ beads_dir }}'
export TMPDIR='{{ scratch_dir }}/{{ step }}'
mkdir -p "$TMPDIR"
{% if teammate %}atm task start {{ step }} "plan-hardening step {{ step }}"
{% endif %}bd update {{ step }} --claim --actor {{ actor }}
bd show {{ step }} --json | jq -er '.[0].description'
```

2. Read the plan when the step reviews it:

```bash
set -euo pipefail
bd show {{ root }} --json | jq -e '.[0] | {id, title, description, design, acceptance_criteria}'
bd list --parent {{ root }} -l stage:sprint -n 0 --json \
  | jq -e '[.[] | {id, title, description, design, acceptance_criteria, metadata}]'
```

3. Do what the step description says. In a fix or verify step enumerate every
   filing reported by earlier steps before filtering the fix beads:

```bash
bd list -l phase-{{ phase }},stage:plan-hardening --include-ephemeral --all -n 0 --json |
  jq -r --arg root '{{ root }}' '.[] | select(.metadata.root == $root) |
    (.close_reason // "") | (try capture("(?:^|[[:space:]])filed=(?<ids>[^[:space:]]+)").ids catch empty) |
    select(. != "none") | split(",")[]'
```

   Compare these IDs with `bd list --parent {{ root }} -l stage:plan-fix
   --all -n 0 --json`; if any are absent, fail and report the missing IDs.
   Every applied fix must cite its finding and a REQ, ADR or existing plan
   source; otherwise rebut it as `unsupported by <source>`. A source is a
   REQ, ADR, or existing plan line, not the finding itself. For a ceremony
   finding use the repository ceremony rule it cites: it can remove only
   process artifacts (records, ledgers, sign-offs with no consumer), never
   product behavior or a test criterion. Never add or remove scope without
   such a source. Verify each source exists and actually supports the claim;
   reopen a finding when a fix or rebuttal cites the wrong rule. In a fix step, the planner is the lead in
   planner modes and rules on every
   finding, including conflicts between findings. Apply an edge or sprint a
   finding justifies; rebut only with a cited reason. If a supported
   finding requires structural changes (including sprints, contracts, ADRs,
   sanity beads or edges), report it to the planner in planner modes or the
   phase lead in lead-adversarial mode. The planner owns and applies the
   structural correction before continuing. In planner modes the planner rules:
   apply a supported fix, rebut with a cited source, or record an `open decision:`
   note explaining a supported finding that cannot be resolved with permitted
   writes. Leave that finding open for the Plan Gate and continue the step; do
   not return "needs lead ruling" to the same planner runner.
   Before planning a sprint or structural fix, read `.claude/skills/atm-beads/resources/planning.md` sections "Sprint Container" (sprint fields, layer,
   and pr_target) and "New Ids", and `.claude/skills/atm-beads/resources/atm-beads-plan-guidelines.md` sections "Ownership And Dependency Relations"
   and "Sprint Bead Shape". Apply their rules to each changed container.
   Never run `bd delete`. To drop or merge a sprint, move its supported scope
   into another container and close the old container with a reason, or rebut
   the finding with a cited reason. Plan-hardening fixes never write ADR or REQ
   files. For a missing ADR or REQ, plan the deliverable in its owning sprint
   (usually the contract sprint), using a `NEW:` id in `metadata.adrs` for
   an ADR as specified by atm-beads `planning.md` New Ids; alternatively
   rebut with a cited existing ADR or requirement.

Before a fix step closes PASS, run this check. Every finding handled by this fix must have
exactly one `fixed:`, `rebutted:`, or `open decision:` note; the latter
must remain open. Partial or mixed dispositions fail:

```bash
set -euo pipefail
bd list --parent '{{ root }}' -l stage:plan-fix --all -n 0 --json |
  jq -r '.[].id' | while IFS= read -r id; do
    bd show "$id" --json | jq -e '.[0] | (.status != "closed" or ((.notes // "") | test("(^|\\n)[[:space:]]*open decision:"; "i") | not)) and (.notes | type == "string" and
      ([test("(^|\\n)[[:space:]]*fixed:[[:space:]]*[^[:space:]]"; "i"), test("(^|\\n)[[:space:]]*rebutted:[[:space:]]*[^[:space:]]"; "i"), test("(^|\\n)[[:space:]]*open decision:[[:space:]]*[^[:space:]]"; "i")] | map(select(.)) | length == 1) and
      (test("in part|partial"; "i") | not))' >/dev/null || {
        echo "finding $id lacks a valid disposition" >&2; exit 1;
      }
  done
```

For an approved new sprint, the planner prepares `SPRINT_VARS` as the JSON
variables for `sprint-bead.json.j2`, `SPRINT_NAME` as its name and
`DEPS_JSON` as a JSON array of predecessor sprint names. From the workdir,
with the supplied BEADS_DIR and TMPDIR, run these commands:

```bash
set -euo pipefail
PLAN_INDEX='{{ plan_index }}'
if [ "$PLAN_INDEX" = integration ]; then
  PLAN_INDEX=$(.claude/skills/atm-beads/scripts/phase-index-path --root '{{ root }}')
fi
sc-compose render --root '{{ workdir }}/.claude/skills/atm-beads/templates' \
  --strict --file sprint-bead.json.j2 --var-file "$SPRINT_VARS" --output "$TMPDIR/sprint.json"
jq -c . "$TMPDIR/sprint.json" > "$TMPDIR/sprint.jsonl"
bd import -i "$TMPDIR/sprint.jsonl"
jq -cn --arg sprint "$SPRINT_NAME" --argjson depends_on "$DEPS_JSON" \
  '{sprint:$sprint,depends_on:$depends_on}' >> "$PLAN_INDEX"
# Do not pour groups during hardening; pour once after the final formula passes.
```

For a changed prerequisite of an existing sprint, update the index first;
then add `bd dep add <sprint> --blocked-by <prerequisite>`
or remove `bd dep remove <sprint> <prerequisite>`.
Before each write confirm both beads are planned sprint containers and the
edge corresponds exactly to the index change. Check the resulting graph and
run validate-plan. A failed command is an execution failure, not a PASS.

4. File findings when you have any. Write a JSON array to
   `${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/{{ step }}-findings.json`, one object per finding:
   `title`, `description` (the problem and the exact correction, naming the
   bead and field), `severity` (`Blocking`, `Important` or `Minor`) and
   `plan_bead` (the bead it is about). Then run:

```bash
set -euo pipefail
STEP='{{ step }}'
PASS=$(bd show "$STEP" --json | jq -er '.[0].metadata.pass')
jq -e 'type == "array" and all(.[]; type == "object" and
  (.severity | IN("Blocking","Important","Minor")) and
  (.title | type == "string" and length > 0) and
  (.description | type == "string" and length > 0) and
  (.plan_bead | type == "string" and length > 0))' "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/$STEP-findings.json" >/dev/null
jq -ce '.[]' "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/$STEP-findings.json" | while IFS= read -r f; do
  existing=$(bd list --parent '{{ root }}' -l 'stage:plan-fix' --all -n 0 --json \
    | jq -r --arg step "$STEP" --argjson f "$f" '[.[] | select(.metadata.filed_by_step == $step and .title == $f.title and .description == $f.description and .metadata.severity == $f.severity and .metadata.plan_bead == $f.plan_bead) | .id][0] // empty')
  if [[ -n "$existing" ]]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$existing"
    continue
  fi
  bd create "$(jq -er .title <<<"$f")" --parent '{{ root }}' --type task --assignee '{{ planner }}' \
    --no-inherit-labels --labels 'stage:plan-fix' --description "$(jq -er .description <<<"$f")" \
    --metadata "$(jq -c --arg pass "$PASS" --arg step "$STEP" '{pass:$pass, filed_by_step:$step, severity, plan_bead}' <<<"$f")" \
    --actor '{{ actor }}' --json | jq -er '.id'
done | tee "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/$STEP-filed.txt"
EXPECTED=$(jq length "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/$STEP-findings.json")
ACTUAL=$(wc -l < "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/$STEP-filed.txt" | tr -d " ")
test "$EXPECTED" = "$ACTUAL" || { echo "finding filing incomplete: expected $EXPECTED, filed $ACTUAL" >&2; exit 1; }
```

   Close a fix bead you verified with
   `bd close <fix bead> --reason "verified: <what resolves it>" --actor {{ actor }}`;
   note one that is still wrong with
   `bd update <fix bead> --append-notes "still open: <what>" --actor {{ actor }}`.

   A create failure or count mismatch is an execution error: retain the finding
   JSON and return the failure envelope; do not continue until filing is repaired.

5. Close the step only when its work completed. Include `detected=<number>`
   equal to the number of findings in the JSON (0 for non-review steps).
   VERDICT is PASS or FAIL as the description says; a completed review with
   findings is still a successful execution. On an execution error, leave the
   bead open (or reopen it with `bd update {{ step }} --status open --actor
   {{ actor }}`) and return the failure envelope instead of running this close.

```bash
set -euo pipefail
bd close {{ step }} --actor {{ actor }} \
  --reason "VERDICT=<PASS|FAIL> detected=<count> filed=<fix bead ids, comma separated, or none> verified=<closed fix bead ids or none>"
```

{% if teammate -%}
6. On successful execution, close your task with a short report that ends in this fenced JSON, then
   stop: `atm task close {{ step }} completed --stdin`. If the close fails,
   read the error, fix its cause and rerun it.
{% else -%}
6. On successful execution, return only this fenced JSON as your final message. Run no `atm` command.
{% endif %}


```json
{"success": true, "data": {"step": "{{ step }}", "verdict": "PASS|FAIL", "findings": ["<filed fix bead ids>"], "commands": [{"argv": "<command>", "output_path": "<scratch path>", "exit_code": 0}], "bd_writes": ["<bd write>"], "file_writes": ["<written file>"]}, "error": null}
```

If execution failed, return this envelope with the actual error and a
specific recovery action. An unruled finding is recoverable: return it to
the planner for a ruling; never mark that failure non-recoverable.
`recoverable` means the planner can finish with its permitted writes;
an unruled finding always meets that condition. {% if teammate %}Close the task with this fenced report
using `atm task close {{ step }} refused --stdin`; this also applies to a
planner fix step that cannot finish. Do not run the completed close.{% else %}Run no ATM command.{% endif %}

```json
{"success": false, "data": {"step": "{{ step }}", "verdict": null, "findings": [], "commands": [{"argv": "<command>", "output_path": "<scratch path>", "exit_code": 1}], "bd_writes": ["<bd write>"], "file_writes": ["<written file>"]}, "error": {"code": "EXECUTION.STEP_FAILED", "message": "<what failed>", "recoverable": true, "suggested_action": "<specific recovery action>"}}
```
  ]]></instructions>
</plan-hardening-step>
